"""
`Machinery.OnOffConsumer` with the parameter file `presets/HotelLoad/hotel_load_bulk_carrier_60k_100k_dwt_at_sea.toml`: the
auxiliary engine power output of a bulk carrier, 60,000 to 99,999 dwt, at sea, as one aggregate consumer of
410 kW.

**A fleet-average literature value, not a ship.** The number is the auxiliary engine
power output that the Fourth IMO GHG Study 2020 assigns to this ship type and size class
in this operational mode (Table 17, page 68). It is an input of that study's fleet
emission model, one value for every ship of the class. It is not a measurement, not an
electrical load analysis and not a breakdown into consumers. The other three modes of
the same table row are listed in `hotel_load_bulk_carrier_60k_100k_dwt_at_sea.provenance.toml`.

The file sets `NominalPower` to that value in watts, `Kr` to 1 so that the nominal power
is the power drawn, and `CycleAmplitude` to 0 so that the load is steady. The start-up
ramp and the switching lag are those of `OnOffConsumer`. Drive `WorkSignal` with 1 while
the ship is in this mode and 0 otherwise.

A thin wrapper with no equations of its own. Change the value by editing a copy of the
file or by using `OnOffConsumer` directly.
"""
component BulkCarrierHotelLoadAtSea
  extends OnOffConsumerPorts
  core = DyadShip.Machinery.OnOffConsumer(apply "dyad://DyadShip/presets/HotelLoad/hotel_load_bulk_carrier_60k_100k_dwt_at_sea.toml")
relations
  connect(WorkSignal, core.WorkSignal)
  connect(core.y, y)
end
